@*
    A labelled text control. The first component in the product, together with PrimaryButton and
    InlineErrorRegion.

    A REAL <label for>, ALWAYS. Not an aria-label, and never a placeholder standing in for a
    label: a placeholder disappears the moment the field is focused, which is exactly when
    somebody needs to know what they are filling in, and it fails at 1.3.1 and 3.3.2 besides.
    Registration is named FIRST among NFR-9's five gated flows (quality-budgets.md:84), so this is
    the component that sets the pattern every later form copies.

    NO PLACEHOLDER ATTRIBUTE ANYWHERE IN THIS COMPONENT. That is deliberate rather than an
    omission, and it is also why `placeholder` sits in the copy gate's localisable-attribute list.

    THE INVALID STATE IS CARRIED THREE WAYS, none of them colour alone: aria-invalid for assistive
    technology, a heavier border for sight, and the message text itself. WCAG 1.4.1 forbids colour
    as the only carrier, and DESIGN.md reserves --danger for the genuinely irreversible - a
    validation error is the case it explicitly says not to spend it on.

    Focus order follows reading order because the markup order IS the reading order: label, then
    control, then message. Nothing here sets tabindex.
*@

<div class="form-field">
    <label class="form-field__label" for="@FieldId">@Label</label>

    <input class="form-field__control"
           id="@FieldId"
           type="@InputType"
           value="@Value"
           autocomplete="@AutoComplete"
           disabled="@Disabled"
           aria-invalid="@(HasMessage ? "true" : null)"
           aria-describedby="@(HasMessage ? MessageId : null)"
           @oninput="HandleInputAsync" />

    @if (HasMessage)
    {
        <p class="form-field__message" id="@MessageId">@Message</p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The field's own id, which the label points at and the message is keyed to.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string FieldId { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The visible label. Always a resource lookup, never a literal.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Label { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The current value.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Value { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>Raised on every keystroke, so the parent owns the value.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The input type. Only affects the browser's own keyboard and masking; validation is the
    /// server's and the parent's, never this attribute's.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string InputType { get; set; } = "text";

    /// <summary>
    /// The autocomplete token. Not decoration: WCAG 1.3.5 asks for it, and it is what lets a
    /// password manager fill this correctly rather than guessing from the label.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string AutoComplete { get; set; } = "off";

    /// <summary>Hint or error text shown under the control, and referenced by aria-describedby.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Message { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the control is disabled, which it is while a submission is in flight.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    private bool HasMessage => !string.IsNullOrEmpty(Message);

    private string MessageId => FieldId + "-message";

    private Task HandleInputAsync(ChangeEventArgs args) =>
        ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(args.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty);
}
